1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game had always remained in the shadow of the Ruy Lopez and the Italian Game, until Garry Kasparov started playing it successfully in the 1990s. Since then, many players have decided to follow the world champion's example and nowadays it is a popular opening on both professional and amateur level. } exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { Black blocks his bishop, but forces White to do the same. } 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. c4 { Absolutely the main move but we covered all the alternatives in the previous chapters. -KEY- } Nb6 ( 8... Ba6 { is the main line, but I recommend to play the text move, because it is at least as good and requires less memorization. Now the most popular is } 9. Nc3 { which will be covered in the next chapter. Here we will focus on } 9. Nd2 { and have a brief look at } 9. b3 { . } ) 
9. b3 { This is sometimes played, but should transpose to 9. Nd2 } a5 10. Bb2 a4 11. Qe3 ( { This has some independent value so let's check it briefly. Probably White's best is } 11. Nd2 Qe6 { which transposes to 9.Nd2 } ) 
axb3 ( 11... Qe6 { is fine but this one is even stronger because it allows us to displace the White king. } ) 
12. axb3 Rxa1 13. Bxa1 Qb4+ 14. Qc3 $2 ( { Everyone played this move. After } 14. Nd2 Qa3 15. Bc3 Bc5 16. Qd3 d5 $1 { Black is clearly better. Probably the only good move is } 14. Kd1 { which is around equal after } Bc5 { . } ) 
Na4 $1 { A nice way to reposition the knight. } 15. Qxb4 Bxb4+ 16. Kd1 Nc5 { Black has a much better structure, the pawn on b3 is just weak. } 
