1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game had always remained in the shadow of the Ruy Lopez and the Italian Game, until Garry Kasparov started playing it successfully in the 1990s. Since then, many players have decided to follow the world champion's example and nowadays it is a popular opening on both professional and amateur level. } exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 Qe7 { Black blocks his bishop, but forces White to do the same. } 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. c4 { Absolutely the main move but we covered all the alternatives in the previous chapters. } Nb6 { 8...Ba6 is the main line, but I recommend to play the text move, because it is at least as good and requires less memorization. Now the most popular is 9. Nc3 which will be covered in the next chapter. Here we will focus on 9. Nd2 and have a brief look at 9.b3. } 9. Nd2 Qe6 { Black prepares ...Bb4 and ... Ba6. } 10. b3 { White strengthens the c4 pawn and prepares to meet ...Bb4 with Bb2. -KEY- } a5 { The key idea of the variation. The a-pawn march essentially solves all Black's problems. } 11. Bb2 ( 11. Qe3 a4 12. Bb2 Bb4 { transposes to our main line. } ) 
a4 ( 11... Bb4 { is dubious because } 12. a3 Bxd2 13. Qxd2 { is just better for White who got a bishop pair for nothing. Somehow Black can't easily break through. This was played in the 16th game of the World Championship match Kasparov-Karpov in 1990. Kasparov went on to win that game and later the match by one point. } ) 
12. Qe3 { White just wants to develop the bishop and castle. } Bb4 13. Bd3 $6 { Most popular, but not very precise. } d6 ( { Not } 13... d5 14. O-O { and Black has no real play against the c4-pawn. In contrast to 13. Be2, White cannot castle short now. } ) 
14. O-O-O $6 { This is asking for trouble, since Black's play on the queenside is already far-advanced. } axb3 15. axb3 d5 $1 { Now that White has castled long, Black switches to undermining the queenside and avoids a queen trade. Black is better, as White's king is obviously weak. An exemplary line runs as follows: } 16. Qg5 O-O 17. Qh4 g6 18. cxd5 Nxd5 19. Ne4 Qe7 20. Nf6+ Nxf6 21. exf6 Qc5+ 22. Kb1 Re8 23. Qh6 Qf8 24. Qxf8+ Bxf8 $15 { 25...Be6 comes next. White's queenside remains weak even without queens. } 
